    <h2>How to Use These Construction Calculators</h2>
    <p>Every calculator on this page is designed for real construction scenarios — not just textbook math. Enter your project dimensions, choose your units (feet, inches, or yards), and get an instant material estimate you can take to the hardware store or use to validate a contractor's quote.</p>
    <p><strong>For concrete projects:</strong> Start with the <a href="/construction/concrete-calculator">Concrete Calculator</a> if you need a general estimate, or use the <a href="/construction/concrete-slab-calculator">Slab Calculator</a> for flat pours. Always add 10% overage to your order to account for spillage, uneven subgrade, and form waste.</p>
    <p><strong>For home additions:</strong> The <a href="/construction/home-addition-cost-calculator">Home Addition Cost Calculator</a> gives itemized estimates by trade (foundation, framing, mechanical, finish work) based on 2026 national average labor and material costs. Use it to budget your project and evaluate contractor bids.</p>
    <p><strong>All estimates are starting points.</strong> Material costs fluctuate by region and season. Always confirm pricing with local suppliers and get at least three contractor quotes for work over $5,000.</p>
