    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["How do I calculate how much concrete I need?", "Multiply length × width × depth (all in feet) and divide by 27 to get cubic yards. Add 10% for waste. For example, a 10×10 ft patio at 4 inches (0.333 ft) thick = 1.23 cubic yards + 10% = 1.35 cubic yards. Our <a href=\"/construction/concrete-calculator\">Concrete Calculator</a> does this instantly, including bag counts."], ["How much does a home addition cost per square foot in 2026?", "The national average is $80–$200 per square foot depending on region, quality, and type. A basic room addition averages $100/sq ft; a full bathroom addition averages $150–$200/sq ft. High-cost metros like San Francisco or New York can run 1.5–2× the national average."], ["How many 80 lb bags of concrete do I need for a 10x10 slab?", "A 10×10 ft slab at 4 inches thick requires 1.23 cubic yards, or about 55.6 cubic feet. Each 80 lb bag yields approximately 0.60 cubic feet, so you need about 93 bags. Use our <a href=\"/construction/concrete-bag-calculator\">Concrete Bag Calculator</a> to adjust for different thicknesses."], ["What is the minimum concrete footing depth in Ohio?", "Ohio follows IRC frost depth requirements. Most of Ohio requires a minimum footing depth of 36 inches below finished grade (frost depth ranges from 30–42 inches depending on county). Local codes may be more restrictive — always check with your county building department."], ["What is the maximum stair rise allowed by building code?", "Under IRC 2021 (adopted by most US states), the maximum allowable riser height is 8.25 inches and the minimum allowable tread depth is 9 inches. Optimal ergonomic stairs use 7–7.75 inch rise with 10–11 inch tread. Use our <a href=\"/calculators/stair.php\">Stair Calculator</a> to compute exact measurements."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
